# Write findings into the notes

The record of a study is the markdown in the game folder. A chat reply is not a record. The next session does not read this chat.

Before finishing any study of a game:

1. Read [LLMs.md](LLMs.md), then that game's `README.md`. Follow [note-shape.md](note-shape.md) when adding or reshaping notes.
2. Write every finding into the topic file for that subject. Create the topic file when the subject is long enough to continue on its own. A record the program reads is a packed struct, in the shape in [note-shape.md](note-shape.md), so a person can see the record without reassembling it from the prose.
3. Put any address, file, or field you saw and did not read into that game's `gaps.md`, under the same heading as the topic. Remove the row when the topic file records the finding. That file opens with two verdicts, in the shape in [note-shape.md](note-shape.md): whether a reconstruction is possible, and whether a modernised remake can start. The first sentence of each is the answer. When the answer is no, the next sentence is the gap that blocks it.
4. The chat may summarize what was written. Do not end with the result only in the chat.

Notes cite offsets from a base the file records, or file offsets in the program as shipped. They do not cite a machine path, a tool install, a temporary image, or an address that exists only at the base a tool chose.

`__stash/` is the local copy of programs under study. Read it. Do not create or change files there, and do not name it in the notes. Notes go in the game folder. A repeatable extractor goes in `<game>/scripts/`, as in [note-shape.md](note-shape.md). Every other file goes in `<game>/extract/`. Do not paste scenario text or other copyrighted writing into the notes.

The root README is the index of analysed games. Add a row there when a study starts. Do not name the game in any other top-level file.
